body:
Hi! Coming here after tracking the bug and seeing the reference in #3160 which introduced the lines causing mixup. This is very annoying bug.

It is caused here: <https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blame/2adfe36627507ab5e01bc4bc7f6b4ec839a2352c/src/query-builder/transformer/RawSqlResultsToEntityTransformer.ts#L175-L183>

In the `forEach` in `RawSqlResultsToEntityTransformer.transform` `this.transformRawResultsGroup` is called, which in turn calls `this.transformJoins`. The lines above  changes `alias.metadata` as a side effect. It causes subsequent iteration of `forEach` to use incorrect metadata thus construct different entity.

Pseudo code of what is happening (`/src/query-builder/transformer/RawSqlResultsToEntityTransformer.ts`):
```js
function transform(rawGroup, alias) {
  const entities = []

  rawGroup.forEach(raw => {
	// First iteration is **not** affected, resulting `entity` is correct
    const entity = transformRawResultsGroup(raw, alias)
    // Here `alias` is already tampered with and has **incorrect** metadata
	// Subsequent iterations will be incorrectly parsed to invalid Entity

    entities.push(entity)
  }
  
  return entities
}

function transformRawResultsGroup(raw, alias) {
  const entity = alias.metadata.create()

  // Fill entity with data `transformColumns`, `transformJoins`, `transformRelationIds`, `transformRelationCounts`
  transformJoins(raw, entity, alias)

  return entity
}

function transformJoins(raw, entity, alias) {
  const matchingRelation = alias.relations.find(relation => relation.path === this.relationPath)
  if (matchingRelation) {
    // If same path is used by child relation, we **incorrectly** replace metadata
    alias.metadata = matchingRelation
  }
}
```

Hopefully with this explanation, someone who understands why the PR was created in the first place, can fix this issue. For me tampering with `alias` through side effects is a big no already, but I have no knowledge on the problem mentioned PR solves.

I know this is not the _proper_ solution, but after reading the (very helpful) commit messages and tests included in #3160, I'd like to suggest reverting the PR. It solves quite specific issue and was lying around for few years with no single upvote. The bug it introduced is, in my assessment, **breaking** change and a **security risk**.

Consider someone used `class-transformer` to hide sensitive fields with `@Exclude()`. If the relation is mapped to different entity which happens to share the same field - it is now exposed. The leak may be **very difficult to notice**.

@pleerock, @AlexMesser, @imnotjames

issue.body:
typeorm with error -> from version 0.2.33
typeorm ok -> 0.2.32
sqlite

## Issue Description

typeorm 
There is a problem with nested relations:
when I insert 3 nested relations, the model used to result is not correct (correct only in the first element).

### Expected Behavior

```
entity:  Category {
      id: 1,
      variations: [
        Variation {
          id: 1,
          name: 'var 1',
          ingredients: [Array],
        },
        Variation {
          id: 2,
          name: 'var 2,
          ingredients: [Array],
        }
      ]
}

```

### Actual Behavior

```
entity:  Category {
      id: 1,
      variations: [
        Variation {
          id: 1,
          name: 'var 1',
          ingredients: [Array],
        },
        IngredientVariation {
          createdAt: 2021-09-29T06:43:24.000Z,
          updatedAt: 2021-09-29T06:43:24.000Z
        }
      ]
}
```

### Steps to Reproduce

category -> many-to-many variations (target Variation)
variation -> one-to-may ingredients (target IngredientVariation)
ingredient-variation -> many-to-one ingredient (target Ingredient)

`repo(category).findOne(1, { relations: ['variations', 'variations.ingredients', 'variations.ingredients.ingredient'] }
`

body:
I have hit this bug also. It took me some time to find this existing issue. I used the process of version bisection to identify that the bug was introduced in [0.2.33](https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md#0233-2021-06-01). I then identified https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/pull/3160 as the likely culprit for introducing the bug, which led me here.

In my case I have a model as follows (simplified):

``` TypeScript
// Education --(1:N)-- EducationTarget --(N:0/1)-- Association --(1:N)-- AssociationTarget

Education {
  @OneToMany(() => EducationTarget, (et) => et.education)
  targets: EducationTarget[];
}

EducationTarget {
  @ManyToOne(() => Education, (e) => e.targets)
  education: Education;

  @ManyToOne(() => Association, (a) => a.educationTargets)
  association: Association | null;
}

Association {
  @OneToMany(() => EducationTarget, (et) => et.association)
  educationTargets: EducationTarget[];

  @OneToMany(() => AssociationTarget, (at) => at.association)
  targets: AssociationTarget[];
}

AssociationTarget {
  @ManyToOne(() => Association, (a) => a.targets)
  association: Association;
}
```

If I then try to execute a query as follows:

``` TypeScript
const result = await entityManager.findOne(Education, id, {
  relations: ["targets", "targets.association"],
});
```

I get erroneous results like:

```
Education {
  targets: [
    EducationTarget {},
    AssociationTarget {}, // Wrong entity type & properties stripped!
    AssociationTarget {}
  ]
}
```

As mentioned by others, the executed underlying query looks correct, but the mapping back to entities is incorrect and data is lost.

Tagging @amaranth as the author of the PR that looks to have introduced the bug.

From above:
> I know this is not the proper solution, but after reading the (very helpful) commit messages and tests included in #3160, I'd like to suggest reverting the PR. It solves quite specific issue and was lying around for few years with no single upvote. The bug it introduced is, in my assessment, breaking change and a security risk.

In my opinion this sounds reasonable as it seems a fundamental bug was introduced in an incorrect implementation to satisfy an edge case. Perhaps @amaranth could then seek to implement the correct solution.

@pleerock, @AlexMesser, @imnotjames - Any chance we could get that moving?
